{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790517465,"nonce":"KBG2uQVbafoAjgBym1kftTyBpEs=","message":"Scope, 2026-09-27: gpu.api.rs declares 264 native members, and only 7 name an IDL operation (#[idl(\"Interface.op\")]); the rest are the plugin's own, wgpu-shaped surface (valid, destroy, requestAdapterWith, driverInfo, nativeLimit, textureFormatUsages, ...). So the web backend is a mapping from the plugin's members onto the wire, in three kinds:\n(a) members that are a WebGPU operation with the plugin's descriptor for its dictionary (createBuffer, createTexture, beginRenderPass, ...). Annotate them with #[idl] and have bindgen generate the web body: the plugin's descriptor struct into the wire's dictionary (both generated from the same IDL dictionary, so the conversion is generated too), then the encoder call.\n(b) members WebGPU spells differently (a limit read through adapter.limits, a feature through features.has, valid/destroy bookkeeping): hand-written over the wire.\n(c) wgpu-only members (native limits and features, ray tracing, mesh shaders, pipeline caches, driver info): raise 'not available on the web'.\nTransport: a command ring and reply records in shared memory, allocated by the plugin, with the agent found through the browser host (451cc06).\nFirst slice: (a)'s generation for the compute path (adapter, device, buffer, shader, compute pipeline, bind group, encoder, dispatch, submit, mapAsync), then a compute example end to end.","files":null}]}